      "math_academy_id": "q-72460",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ and $f(2)=-3,$ what is the value of\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}{({f}^{2}(x)-3x)}^{3}\\,?\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=f(2)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{({f}^{2}(x)-3x)}^{3} & ={({f}^{2}(2)-3\\cdot 2)}^{3} \\\\  & ={((-3)^{2}-3\\cdot 2)}^{3} \\\\  & =(9-6)^{3} \\\\  & ={3}^{3} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
